From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 06:28:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: apple: t8132-j604: Add the J604 power-domain
 policy

The J713 states which T8132 power domains Linux owns in
t8132-j713-pmgr.dtsi: qualified domains are enabled, retained core,
GPIO and display domains are firmware-owned, and the rest stay
unregistered. The J604 has only one line of that policy, the ps_pmc
override from df99bbcc4c. Every other domain was left to the SoC
default.

Add t8132-j604-pmgr.dtsi in the same form, covering all 129 T8132
domains. Each domain takes the J713 decision with two kinds of
exception:
- Domains an enabled J604 node consumes, directly or as a parent, stay
  enabled. These are atc3_common, atc3_usb_aon, atc3_usb, apcie_gp,
  apcie_sys_gp, apcie_phy_sw, nub_spmi0, nub_spmi_a0 and nub_spmi_a1.
- The dispext0/1 sys, fe and cpu domains are disabled rather than
  firmware-owned, because they are off when the J604 boots (genpd on
  the 10.1 boot) and no J604 node consumes them.

ps_pmc stays unregistered, now as part of the whole policy. ps_gfx
stays disabled here and t8132-j604-gpu.dtsi enables it with its gfx_tvm
parent, as on the J713.

The policy was generated from the J713 file, the J604 device tree and
the J604 boot-time genpd state. The generator and its per-domain
derivation are kept with the J604 bring-up notes.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
